    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Are you—or someone you know—worried about memory loss? Don’t worry. There are steps you can take to keep your mind "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". However, not everything you’ve heard about memory is "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Here are some popular myths about the human brain."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "m1",
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Myth 1 Doing puzzles will improve your memory."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Fact: The benefits of doing puzzles are limited. A puzzle "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " strengthens the mental skills it uses. If you play "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Sudoku",
            "zh": "数独游戏"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " a lot, for example, you’ll get better at Sudoku. It won’t help you remember people’s names better. The benefits only seem to last as long as you continue doing the puzzles. If you "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", you could lose whatever benefits you gained."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "m2",
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Myth 2 Exercise doesn’t help improve your memory."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Fact: Exercise helps. Activities like walking and running can improve blood flow to your brain. They also require you to "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". For example, you need to get familiar with new environments and keep count of your "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "repetitions",
            "zh": "重复的动作"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "m3",
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Myth 3 We only use 10 percent of our brains."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Fact: We use almost all parts of our brains almost all the time. However, scientists only "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " about 10 percent of how the human brain works."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "m4",
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Myth 4 Our memory banks are "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Fact: Our memory has a limited amount of space to store information. We’re human. Our brains are sometimes compared to computers, but their neural pathways can only hold so much information at once. This is why learning things step by step is more "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " than trying to "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "cram",
            "zh": "硬塞"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " everything into one night. It prevents our brains from feeling full."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "m5",
        "type": "heading",
        "level": 3,
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Myth 5 Stored memories stay the same over time."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "f5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Fact: Our memories aren’t set in stone. They can be "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Even important things that we believe are burnt into our memory can change over time. This is why we should regularly "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " key ideas and facts after learning something new."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
